\title{aciR: Assimilative Causal Inference for Conditional Gaussian Nonlinear
Systems}
\description{
The aciR package implements assimilative causal inference (ACI) for
conditional Gaussian nonlinear systems (CGNS). A CGNS pairs an observed
signal with an unobserved component whose conditional statistics, given the
observed path, are Gaussian and available in closed form.
}
\details{
Most analyses need three calls. \code{\link[=aci_dyad_model]{aci_dyad_model()}} or \code{\link[=aci_cgns_model]{aci_cgns_model()}}
builds an \code{aci_model}, \code{\link[=aci_simulate]{aci_simulate()}} draws a realisation of it, and
\code{\link[=aci]{aci()}} runs the whole workflow on an observed signal and returns an \code{aci}
object with \code{\link[=summary.aci]{summary.aci()}}, \code{\link[=plot.aci]{plot.aci()}} and \code{\link[=as.data.frame.aci]{as.data.frame.aci()}}
methods.

Beneath that sits the numerical core: the forward filter (\code{\link[=aci_filter]{aci_filter()}}),
the backward smoother (\code{\link[=aci_smoother]{aci_smoother()}}) and the relative-entropy
causal-information metric (\code{\link[=aci_metric]{aci_metric()}}). The three are written against a
general CGNS components list rather than a specific model, so they apply to
any conditional Gaussian system; \link{aci_components} documents that schema and
\code{\link[=aci_dyad_components]{aci_dyad_components()}} is its worked example.

The numerical core is graded against fixtures generated by an independent
MATLAB harness reproducing the deterministic core of the reference
implementation. The \emph{Validation and the independent oracle} vignette
records the design, the provenance and the observed agreement; the
\emph{Assumptions and interpretation} vignette states what the method assumes and
what an ACI peak does and does not support.

The method reimplemented here is due to Andreou, Chen and Bollt (2026).
}
